        Case: case101_prim_onehot_multihot_suppressed
        Original method: spec_plus_rtl
        Allowed signals:
        clk, rst_n, oh_i, addr_i, en_i, err_o

        Specification:
        When more than one bit of oh_i is high in the same cycle, err_o must assert in that same cycle.

        Original assertion:
        assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) ((oh_i[3] && oh_i[2] && oh_i[1] && oh_i[0])) |-> (err_o));

        Locked implication operator:
        |->

        Locked consequent:
        err_o

        Why the original antecedent failed to trigger:
        - Original antecedent: (oh_i[3] && oh_i[2] && oh_i[1] && oh_i[0])
        - Diagnosis: Original antecedent required all four bits of oh_i to be high at once, which never occurs in the buggy trace.

        Trigger condition extracted from the good/bad traces:
        - The bug-revealing condition is an enabled multi-hot request: en_i is high and more than one bit of oh_i is high in the same sampled cycle. The observed primary trace injects 4'b1010 and 4'b0110, while held-out uses different multi-hot literals. The repaired antecedent should generalize the multi-hot relation instead of matching one specific literal.
        - First divergence cycle: 2
        - First divergence values: err_o: good=1 bad=0

        Repair rules:
        1) Repair only the antecedent. We will reuse the locked implication operator and locked consequent exactly as given.
        2) Use only allowed signals.
        3) Do not include !rst_n in the antecedent because the assertion already has disable iff (!rst_n).
        4) Prefer an antecedent that actually fires on the bug-revealing scenario in the observed buggy trace.
        5) Preserve correctness on the good trace if possible.
        6) Do not hardcode one observed literal unless the spec or RTL independently justifies it.
        7) If the consequent is checked on the next cycle (|->), make the antecedent describe the precondition of that next-cycle effect.

